      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been looking at some cases where the lesions are almost... pebbled and irregular, like they have a very distinct, uneven texture that doesn't seem to follow normal dermatological progressions. In these cases where the texture itself is the most striking part of the morphology, is there a specific subset of L-codes (Diseases of the skin and subcutaneous tissue) that we should be prioritizing over general inflammatory codes? I want to avoid any ambiguity in the claim.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the texture—the \"pebbled and irregular\" quality—is the most dominant feature, you should pivot away from general inflammatory codes and look directly into the specific morphological descriptions within the L-codes. You're looking for codes that address *texture* or *surface changes*, rather than just redness or inflammation.\n\nSpecifically, explore categories related to keratoses, scaly conditions, or disorders of skin texture, as these often capture the uneven, bumpy nature you are describing. If the texture is highly irregular and suggests a specific type of epidermal change, codes under disorders of the epidermis might be more appropriate than codes for general rashes.\n\nIt is crucial to document the texture in the clinical notes, as this qualitative detail is what will justify moving away from a generic code. If the texture strongly suggests something like xerosis or a specific form of ichthyosis, those subsets of L-codes are your best bet.",
      "source": "target",
